                <pb facs="0385" n="273" sameAs="frus:frus1910/pg273"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 5315/813.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador Bacon to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Paraphrase—Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1910-03-02"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">March 2,
                    1910</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Bacon states that the foreign office has not yet received a reply from
                    England, but in answer to his specific question said that he might inform his
                    Government that France would be perfectly <pb facs="0386" n="274" xml:id="pg274"
                    /> satisfied to accept proposition to concede to France 100 kilometers Ch’eng-tu
                    extension, England to satisfy them as to subengineer Hankow-Canton, France to
                    have westernmost division of Ch’eng-tu extension.</p>
            </div>
